INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, quiz_id, question, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at) VALUES (
  'Q_GTU_PHYSICS_2024_001',
  'quiz_BE01R00021_u1',
  'In Newton''s rings experiment viewed by reflected monochromatic light of wavelength $\lambda$, the central spot is always dark because:',
  '[{"id":"A","text":"An additional phase change of $\\pi$ radians (path difference of $\\lambda/2$) occurs upon reflection at the rarer-to-denser medium boundary"},{"id":"B","text":"The film thickness at the point of contact is equal to $\\lambda/4$"},{"id":"C","text":"Destructive interference occurs due to absorption of light by the plano-convex lens"},{"id":"D","text":"Refraction through the glass plate introduces a path difference of $\\lambda$"}]',
  0,
  'According to Stokes'' treatment, when light is reflected from the boundary of a denser medium (glass plate with higher refractive index), it undergoes a phase shift of $\pi$ radians, equivalent to a path difference of $\lambda/2$. At the point of contact, film thickness $t = 0$, so the net effective path difference $\Delta = 2\mu t + \lambda/2 = \lambda/2$, which satisfies the condition for destructive interference (dark fringe).',
  '2026-08-26 19:23:43'
);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, quiz_id, question, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at) VALUES (
  'Q_GTU_PHYSICS_2023_002',
  'quiz_BE01R00021_u1',
  'In Newton''s rings experiment using light of wavelength $\lambda$, the diameter $D_n$ of the $n^{\text{th}}$ dark ring in reflected light is directly proportional to:',
  '[{"id":"A","text":"$\\sqrt{n}$"},{"id":"B","text":"$n$"},{"id":"C","text":"$n^2$"},{"id":"D","text":"$\\sqrt{2n - 1}$"}]',
  0,
  'The diameter of the $n^{\text{th}}$ dark ring in reflected light is given by $D_n = \sqrt{4 n R \lambda} = 2\sqrt{n R \lambda}$, where $R$ is the radius of curvature of the plano-convex lens. Therefore, $D_n \propto \sqrt{n}$ (proportional to the square root of natural numbers). For bright rings, diameter is proportional to $\sqrt{2n - 1}$.',
  '2026-08-26 19:23:43'
);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, quiz_id, question, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at) VALUES (
  'Q_GTU_PHYSICS_2024_003',
  'quiz_BE01R00021_u1',
  'A plane transmission diffraction grating has $N$ lines per centimeter. What is the maximum wavelength of light $\lambda$ for which the first-order principal maximum ($n=1$) can be observed at normal incidence?',
  '[{"id":"A","text":"$\\lambda_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{N}$"},{"id":"B","text":"$\\lambda_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2N}$"},{"id":"C","text":"$\\lambda_{\\text{max}} = N$"},{"id":"D","text":"$\\lambda_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{2}{N}$"}]',
  0,
  'The grating equation at normal incidence is $(a + b)\sin\theta = n\lambda$, where the grating element $(a + b) = 1/N$. For the first order ($n=1$), $\lambda = \frac{1}{N}\sin\theta$. Since the maximum possible value of $\sin\theta$ is 1 (at $\theta = 90^\circ$), the maximum observable wavelength is $\lambda_{\text{max}} = 1/N$.',
  '2026-08-26 19:23:43'
);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, quiz_id, question, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at) VALUES (
  'Q_GTU_PHYSICS_2024_004',
  'quiz_BE01R00021_u1',
  'In an air wedge film illuminated by sodium light of wavelength $\lambda = 6000\text{ \AA}$, consecutive dark fringes are separated by fringe width $\beta = 0.12\text{ mm}$. What is the wedge angle $\theta$ in radians?',
  '[{"id":"A","text":"$2.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ rad}$"},{"id":"B","text":"$5.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ rad}$"},{"id":"C","text":"$1.25 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ rad}$"},{"id":"D","text":"$2.5 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ rad}$"}]',
  0,
  'Fringe width in a wedge-shaped film is given by $\beta = \frac{\lambda}{2\mu \theta}$. For an air wedge ($\mu = 1$), $\theta = \frac{\lambda}{2\beta} = \frac{6000 \times 10^{-10}\text{ m}}{2 \times (0.12 \times 10^{-3}\text{ m})} = \frac{6 \times 10^{-7}}{2.4 \times 10^{-4}} = 2.5 \times 10^{-3}\text{ radians}$.',
  '2026-08-26 19:23:43'
);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, quiz_id, question, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at) VALUES (
  'Q_GTU_PHYSICS_2023_005',
  'quiz_BE01R00021_u1',
  'In Fraunhofer diffraction at a single slit of width $a$ illuminated by light of wavelength $\lambda$, the condition for the $m^{\text{th}}$ minima is:',
  '[{"id":"A","text":"$a \\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\quad (m = \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\dots)$"},{"id":"B","text":"$a \\sin\\theta = (2m+1)\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$"},{"id":"C","text":"$a \\cos\\theta = m\\lambda$"},{"id":"D","text":"$a \\tan\\theta = m\\lambda$"}]',
  0,
  'For Fraunhofer single slit diffraction, the path difference between secondary wavelets from the top and bottom edges of the slit is $a \sin\theta$. When $a \sin\theta = m\lambda$ with $m \in \{\pm 1, \pm 2, \dots\}$, the slit can be divided into $2m$ equal zones whose wavelets cancel in pairs, producing complete destructive interference (minima).',
  '2026-08-26 19:23:43'
);
